ace the `sorry` block. The conditions in `radius_add_one_le_card_compl` are being verified, specifically, the choice of the ball.\n\n\n**Verifying Graph Properties**\n\nFocus shifted to demonstrating that for any Roman dominating function, the inequality `radius(G) + 1 ≤ romanWeight(f)` holds. Recognizing that this inequality is a restatement of existing mathematical knowledge, considered a proof based on first principles. The proof is contingent on the definition of `radius(G)`.\n\n**Applying Definitions Directly**\n\nEstablished that the radius is the minimum eccentricity of a vertex. The goal is to prove `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f`. Realized that it suffices to find one vertex `c` for which the ball around `c` with radius `romanWeight(f) - 1` is the entire graph.\n\n**Constructing the Proof Structure**\n\nA potential proof structure involves showing that there *exists* a vertex `c` such that `eccentricity(G, c) = radius G` and then demonstrate `eccentricity(G, c) + 1 ≤ romanWeight f`. This leverages the definition of the radius in relation to vertex eccentricity. The next step is to examine the ball centered at $c$, $B_{romanWeight(f)-1}(c)$.\n\n**Establishing Validity of Inequality**\n\nRecognized the crucial link between `eccentricity(c) ≤ romanWeight(f) - 1` and `B_{romanWeight(f)-1}(c) = univ`.  If eccentricity condition holds, the ball condition also holds, which can be rearranged to the original goal of the radius plus 1 bounded above by the roman weight.\n\n**Direct Reasoning from Radius**\n\nBy definition of radius, demonstrated that for a vertex $c$ where $eccentricity(c) = radius(G)$, if $eccentricity(c) \\le romanWeight(f) - 1$, then by definition $B_{romanWeight(f)-1}(c) = univ$. Consequently, $radius(G) \\le romanWeight(f) - 1$, which is the main goal.\n\n**Rephrasing Goal for Formalization**\n\nThe problem has been rephrased: Prove that for every connected graph G and for every Roman dominating function $f$, $radius(G) + 1 ≤ romanWeight(f)$. If the goal can be converted to Lean 4, then the `sorry` block will be removed.\n\n**Exploring Formalization of Logic**\n\nConsidered how to formulate the statement \"For every connected graph G and every Roman dominating function f\" within the Lean 4 environment. Identified that such formalization relies on the appropriate type classes or structures for connected graphs and Roman dominating functions.\n\n**Implementing Proof Strategy**\n\nFocused on formalizing the proof strategy. If `radius(G) <= romanWeight(f) - 1`, this implies `radius G + 1 <= romanWeight f`.  This inequality will be the core of the proof.\n\n**Preparing Proof Construction**\n\nPreparing a Lean 4 Lemma that will capture `radius G + 1 <= romanWeight f`, for all graphs and roman dominating functions. The Lemma will define all variables, and then the logic can be applied to replace the `sorry` statement.\n\n\n**Analyzing Ball Properties for Proof**\n\nConsidered using the lemma `ballWithin_univ_or_card_ge`. Determined that either `B_{romanWeight(f) - 1}(v) = univ` or `|B_{romanWeight(f) - 1}(v)| ≥ romanWeight(f)`. If at least one vertex's ball equals the universe, the proof is immediate. Explored the case where all balls are not the universe, leading to `radius(G) + 1 > romanWeight(f)` which would create a contradiction.\n\n**Connecting Ball Cardinality and Proof Goal**\n\nInvestigated under what conditions `B_m(v) = univ`. Recognized `B_{k + m}(v) = univ` if `|V| ≤ |B_k(v)| + m` using `ballWithin_eq_univ_of_card_add`. Chose `m = |V| - |B_k(v)|` leading to `eccentricity(v) ≤ k + |V| - |B_k(v)|`.\n\n**Establishing Ball Cardinality Condition**\n\nTo achieve the goal, focused on finding a `v` and `k` with `k + |V| - |B_k(v)| ≤ romanWeight(f) - 1`. This leads to `|B_k(v)| ≥ |V| - romanWeight(f) + k + 1`.\n\n**Examining Neighborhood Relationships**\n\nConsidered the neighborhood of two vertices u1 and u2 labeled 2, observing every vertex with label 0 is in the neighborhood of $u_1$ or $u_2$. Explored relationships between the neighborhoods of these vertices to achieve the proof goal.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/check.sh","ContentOffset":0,"EndLine":100,"StartLine":1,"toolAction":"Viewing check.sh","toolSummary":"View check.sh script"}}]}
{"step_index":1337,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:46:06Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:46:06+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:46:06+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity/check.sh`\nTotal Lines: 51\nTotal Bytes: 2182\nShowing lines 1 to 51\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/bin/bash\n2: # check.sh — the ONLY command an agent may run.\n3: #\n4: #   /abs/path/tools/antigravity/check.sh FILE.lean\n5: #\n6: # Lives outside every agent workspace and is hashed by guard.sh: agents can\n7: # write inside their workspace, so a checker kept there could be rewritten into\n8: # anything. It screens the file first (tools/antigravity_handoff.py lint), because\n9: # Lean elaboration can itself run shell commands; then elaborates it with bare\n10: # `lean` and an explicit LEAN_PATH, and prints every theorem's axioms. Nothing\n11: # here depends on $HOME — agents run under a private one — and `lake` is never\n12: # involved (in this project lake deletes Mathlib when it cannot re-clone).\n13: set -uo pipefail\n14: f=\"${1:?usage: check.sh FILE.lean}\"\n15: [ -f \"$f\" ] || { echo \"CHECK: no such file: $f\"; exit 2; }\n16: \n17: ROOT=\"$(cd \"$(dirname \"$(readlink -f \"$0\")\")/../..\" && pwd)\"\n18: # Tool paths come from a protected file, not from the home directory: agents run\n19: # under a private HOME, and on compute nodes `getent passwd` reports a different\n20: # home (/home/<user>@ad…) than the one the tools live in.\n21: ENVF=\"$ROOT/handoff/.agf_env\"\n22: export ELAN_HOME=\"$(grep -m1 '^AGF_ELAN_HOME=' \"$ENVF\" 2>/dev/null | cut -d= -f2-)\"\n23: LEAN=\"$ELAN_HOME/bin/lean\"\n24: [ -x \"$LEAN\" ] || { echo \"CHECK: lean not found (ELAN_HOME='$ELAN_HOME' from $ENVF)\"; exit 2; }\n25: PY=\"$ROOT/.venv/bin/python\"\n26: LP_FILE=\"$ROOT/handoff/.agf_lean_path\"\n27: [ -s \"$LP_FILE\" ] || { echo \"CHECK: missing $LP_FILE\"; exit 2; }\n28: export LEAN_PATH=\"$(cat \"$LP_FILE\")\"\n29: \n30: orig=\"$(ls \"$ROOT\"/benchmark/challenges_*/\"$(basename \"$f\")\" 2>/dev/null | head -1)\"\n31: if ! \"$PY\" \"$ROOT/tools/antigravity_handoff.py\" lint ${orig:+--original \"$orig\"} \"$f\"; then\n32:   echo \"CHECK: refused to elaborate — remove the construct named above.\"\n33:   exit 3\n34: fi\n35: \n36: tmp=\"$(mktemp --suffix=.lean)\"\n37: cp \"$f\" \"$tmp\"\n38: for t in $(grep -oE '^theorem +[A-Za-z0-9_]+' \"$f\" | awk '{print $2}'); do\n39:   echo \"#print axioms $t\" >> \"$tmp\"\n40: done\n41: timeout 1800 \"$LEAN\" \"$tmp\"\n42: rc=$?\n43: rm -f \"$tmp\"\n44: if [ $rc -eq 0 ]; then\n45:   echo \"CHECK: elaborated. Allowed axioms: propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound.\"\n46:   echo \"       'sorryAx' in a list above means that theorem is unfinished.\"\n47: else\n48:   echo \"CHECK: FAILED (exit $rc)\"\n49: fi\n50: exit $rc\n51: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":1338,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:46:06Z","thinking":"**Examining script's behavior**\n\nThe script first lints the input file using a Python tool. It then extracts theorem names from the input file and prints their axioms to a temporary file, which is then passed to a theorem prover with a timeout. The acceptance criteria specify successful execution of `check.sh` and the unique activation of a specific proof.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"view_file","args":{"AbsolutePath":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py","ContentOffset":0,"EndLine":100,"StartLine":1,"toolAction":"Viewing linter","toolSummary":"View antigravity_handoff.py lines 1-100"}}]}
{"step_index":1339,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:46:09Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:46:09+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:46:09+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/tools/antigravity_handoff.py`\nTotal Lines: 348\nTotal Bytes: 15551\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: #!/usr/bin/env python\n2: \"\"\"tools/antigravity_handoff.py — run the prover benchmark through Antigravity.\n3: \n4: Antigravity's `/teamwork-preview` is a slash command inside its IDE, not an API,\n5: so the integration is a file handoff: export the challenges, run the team over\n6: them there, drop what comes back into a directory, verify it here.\n7: \n8: The verification deliberately reuses the *same* path as the seven-model\n9: campaign — `LeanSubprocessProver._run_lean(..., audit_axioms=True)` over a\n10: challenge file whose `prove`/`refute` declarations are two directions of one\n11: `Candidate` — so the result is directly comparable to that baseline rather than\n12: being a differently-scored number.\n13: \n14: Why that matters here specifically: every formalization bug this repository has\n15: found produced an *apparent success*. A model \"proved\" three items that were\n16: `Fin 0` artefacts; another exploited `minCard`'s 0 fallback on an undefined\n17: invariant. A stronger agent team is more likely to find such holes, not less, so\n18: nothing is counted as resolved unless the kernel accepts it under an axiom\n19: audit, and `--strict` additionally re-checks that the accepted direction agrees\n20: with the ground-truth label the benchmark carries.\n21: \n22: Usage:\n23:     python tools/antigravity_handoff.py export --partition gold --out handoff/\n24:     # ... run /teamwork-preview over handoff/, save results into handoff/solved/\n25:     python tools/antigravity_handoff.py verify --partition gold --dir handoff/solved\n26: \"\"\"\n27: from __future__ import annotations\n28: \n29: import argparse\n30: import collections\n31: import json\n32: import os\n33: import re\n34: import sys\n35: \n36: sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))))\n37: \n38: from config import CONFIG  # noqa: E402\n39: \n40: BRIEF = \"\"\"\\\n41: # AutoGraphForge — prover benchmark handoff\n42: \n43: Each `Challenge_<id>.lean` states one conjecture once, as `Candidate`, and asks\n44: for **either** direction:\n45: \n46:     theorem prove_<id>  :   Candidate := by sorry\n47:     theorem refute_<id> : ¬ Candidate := by sorry\n48: \n49: Close exactly one, editing the file in place and keeping its name. Leave the\n50: other declaration untouched, `sorry` and all — the verifier removes it.\n51: \n52: What makes a result count:\n53: \n54: * Nothing above the first `theorem` may change: imports, options, `Candidate`.\n55:   Helper lemmas go after `Candidate`, as top-level `theorem`/`lemma`/`def`.\n56: * The chosen theorem keeps its exact name and type. The verifier re-checks the\n57:   type by elaborating `_root_.prove_<id> : _root_.Candidate` itself.\n58: * No `sorry`, `admit`, `axiom`, `native_decide`, `namespace`, `section`,\n59:   `variable`, custom `notation`/`macro`/`syntax`, or metaprogramming; only\n60:   `propext`, `Classical.choice` and `Quot.sound` may appear among the axioms.\n61: * Never run `lake` in this project — it deletes Mathlib.\n62: \n63: `decide` evaluates the invariants on an explicit small graph\n64: (`GraphCalc.ofEdges n [(0, 1), ...]`), so a refutation is usually a witness plus\n65: `decide`. Ground truth is withheld. {n} items in this partition.\n66: \"\"\"\n67: \n68: \n69: def _index(bench: str, partition: str):\n70:     path = os.path.join(bench, f\"challenges_{partition}\", \"index.json\")\n71:     if not os.path.exists(path):\n72:         raise SystemExit(f\"no challenge index at {path} — run \"\n73:                          f\"tools/prover_eval/challenge.py --partition {partition}\")\n74:     return json.load(open(path)), os.path.dirname(path)\n75: \n76: \n77: def do_export(args) -> int:\n78:     index, src = _index(args.benchmark, args.partition)\n79:     os.makedirs(args.out, exist_ok=True)\n80:     os.makedirs(os.path.join(args.out, \"solved\"), exist_ok=True)\n81:     import shutil\n82:     for item in index:\n83:         shutil.copy(os.path.join(src, item[\"file\"]), os.path.join(args.out, item[\"file\"]))\n84:     with open(os.path.join(args.out, \"README.md\"), \"w\") as fh:\n85:         fh.write(BRIEF.format(n=len(index)))\n86:     # The labels stay on this side of the handoff.\n87:     with open(os.path.join(args.out, \".ground_truth.json\"), \"w\") as fh:\n88:         json.dump({i[\"id\"]: {\"label\": i[\"label\"], \"witness_g6\": i.get(\"witness_g6\"),\n89:                              \"statement\": i[\"statement\"]} for i in index}, fh, indent=1)\n90:     print(f\"exported {len(index)} challenges → {args.out}\")\n91:     print(f\"  brief:       {os.path.join(args.out, 'README.md')}\")\n92:     print(f\"  put results in: {os.path.join(args.out, 'solved')}\")\n93:     return 0\n94: \n95: \n96: def _candidate_of(text: str):\n97:     \"\"\"The `abbrev Candidate : Prop := …` body, normalised for comparison.\"\"\"\n98:     m = re.search(r\"abbrev\\s+Candidate\\s*:\\s*Prop\\s*:=(.*?)(?=\\n\\ntheorem )\", text, re.S)\n99:     return re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", m.group(1)).strip() if m else None\n100: \nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
